  "explanation": "(a) Cumulative frequency table:\n\n| Marks (%) | $f$ | Upper class boundary | Cumulative frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $0-9$ | $7$ | $9.5$ | $7$ |\n| $10-19$ | $11$ | $19.5$ | $18$ |\n| $20-29$ | $17$ | $29.5$ | $35$ |\n| $30-39$ | $20$ | $39.5$ | $55$ |\n| $40-49$ | $29$ | $49.5$ | $84$ |\n| $50-59$ | $34$ | $59.5$ | $118$ |\n| $60-69$ | $30$ | $69.5$ | $148$ |\n| $70-79$ | $25$ | $79.5$ | $173$ |\n| $80-89$ | $21$ | $89.5$ | $194$ |\n| $90-99$ | $6$ | $99.5$ | $200$ |\n\nThe curve (ogive) is drawn in the solution diagram: plot cumulative frequency against upper class boundary and join with a smooth curve.\n\n(b)(i) $95\\%$ of $200 = 190$ candidates. Reading across from cumulative frequency $190$ gives a mark of about $87.5$ (the key reads $79.5 + 8 = 87.5$). (ii) Reading up from a mark of $45$, the cumulative frequency is about $69$ (the key's reading), so $P = \\frac{69}{200} = 0.345 \\approx 0.35$.\n\n**Answers:** (b)(i) $\\approx 87.5$ marks; (ii) $\\approx 0.35$. (Key note: the key labels '0.345 ≈ 0.35' as '1 d.p.'; it is 2 d.p. The exact read-off depends on the drawn curve, about $69$ to $71$ candidates.)",
